title: Task kinds
tags: Tasks
kind: reference
summary: The nine kinds of task, which ones run in the background, and which carry a result.

|!Kind |!What |!Started by |
|`reconcile` |a full or scoped reconcile ([[Running a reconcile]]) |`POST …/reconcile` — asynchronous, `202` and a task id |
|`duplicates` |a duplicate scan ([[The duplicate scan]]) |`POST …/duplicates/scan` — asynchronous |
|`relink` |re-homing orphaned metarecords onto the files carrying their content ([[Relinking orphans]]) |`POST …/orphans/relink` — asynchronous |
|`load` |the background half of loading a repository: its mount points, the buffered filesystem events, the watcher |`POST /repos/load` |
|`flush` |the watcher applying a batch of buffered events |the daemon itself |
|`query` |a query being evaluated |`POST …/query` — synchronous |
|`prune` |pruning the log |`POST …/log/prune` — synchronous |
|`backup` |a backup of the store: `copying the store` (every other request waits), then `checking the copy` ([[Backups and restore]]) |`POST …/backup` — synchronous — or the daemon itself, for the automatic one |

The asynchronous kinds deliver their outcome through the task: `result` holds
the reconcile's or the scan's summary, and the client polls until the task is
done. The synchronous kinds return their result with the request's own response;
they are registered only so that other clients can see the repository is busy
and why their own work waits. A coordinated revert (`revert/start`, `commit`,
`abort`) is not a task: its lock is observable on its own ([[Filesystem coordination]]).

`flush` is internal but shown like any other — a client may offer to hide it,
since flushes are frequent (one per quiet period of filesystem activity).

At most one `reconcile` and one `duplicates` task can be active per repository;
a second request is refused (`409`) rather than queued, since it would only redo
the work in progress ([[Task concurrency]]).
